---
// Open source: the licence, how to contribute as CONTRIBUTING.md and the CI gates
// really are, and the repository's live numbers through the shared script.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { siteFacts } from '../lib/repo.mjs';
import { GH_URL, ISSUES_URL, PYPI_URL } from '../lib/site-nav.mjs';

const { version } = siteFacts();

const setup = `$ git clone ${GH_URL}.git
$ cd skep
$ uv sync --frozen
$ make all`;

const gates = `$ TMPDIR=$HOME/.cache/skep-test-tmp uv run --with pytest-xdist --with pytest-cov pytest -q -n auto --cov=skep --cov-report=xml
$ uvx diff-cover coverage.xml --compare-branch=origin/main --fail-under=90
$ uv run ruff check .
$ uv run ruff format --check src/skep tests scripts
$ uv run mypy
$ TMPDIR=$HOME/.cache/skep-test-tmp uv run python scripts/scorecard.py`;
---

<Base
  title="Open Source"
  description="skep is MIT-licensed. How to set up a checkout, which gates a pull request has to pass, and where help is most useful."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap narrow stack">
      <p class="eyebrow t-micro">Open source</p>
      <h1 class="t-h2">MIT-licensed, and readable end to end</h1>
      <p class="t-lead prose-width">
        A supervisor you cannot inspect is another thing to take on trust. The whole boundary is in
        the repository: the sandbox profiles, the policy engine, the re-verification step and the
        tests that pin them.
      </p>
      <div class="row">
        <Button href={GH_URL} icon="github">Source on GitHub</Button>
        <Button href={`${GH_URL}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`} variant="ghost" icon="arrow-up-right">
          CONTRIBUTING.md
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Section band narrow eyebrow="Licence" title="MIT">
    <div class="stack prose-width">
      <p>
        Use it, change it and ship it, for personal or commercial work. The text is in
        <a class="c-green" href={`${GH_URL}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>LICENSE</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small c-mut">
        One exception, and it concerns this website only: four text-animation components in the
        site’s source come from React Bits under MIT with the Commons Clause. They are not part of
        the skep package and are not covered by skep’s licence; see
        <a class="c-green" href={`${GH_URL}/blob/main/NOTICE`}>NOTICE</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    narrow
    eyebrow="Contribute"
    title="Set up a checkout"
    lead="Python 3.12 or newer and uv. The default test suite needs no model, no credentials, no network and no Docker."
  >
    <div class="stack">
      <CodeBlock code={setup} title="Development setup" step={1} />
      <p class="prose-width">
        <code>make all</code> runs lint, type checking and the default test suite. If
        <code>make</code> is not installed, run the commands in step 2 directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    band
    narrow
    eyebrow="Gates"
    tone="green"
    title="What a pull request has to pass"
    lead="CI runs these on Linux and macOS. Run them locally first: all six green."
  >
    <div class="stack">
      <CodeBlock code={gates} title="The six gates" step={2} />
      <div class="prose prose-width">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The suite has no known failures.</strong> A red test is yours, not a
            pre-existing one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Diff coverage, 90%.</strong> The lines your change adds have to be run by a
            test. <code>make all</code> does not check this; CI does.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><code>TMPDIR</code> outside <code>/tmp</code> on Linux.</strong> The sandbox
            tests hide <code>/tmp</code>, so tests that keep their files there fail.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A hygiene scan in CI.</strong> A secrets scanner (gitleaks) runs on the Linux
            leg. A credential-shaped string in a test fixture turns the pull request red.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The macOS leg is narrower.</strong> It runs lint, types, unit tests and the
            smoke test; coverage, the sandbox smoke and the hygiene scan run on Linux only.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The website has its own checks.</strong> A change under <code>site/</code> is
            built and link-checked by a separate workflow; see
            <a href={`${GH_URL}/blob/main/site/README.md`}>site/README.md</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Not needed for a pull request">
        <code>scripts/local-release-gates.sh</code> is the release gate. It adds container builds,
        a wheel build and the scans, and needs <code>make</code>, Docker and gitleaks.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    narrow
    eyebrow="What a change needs"
    title="Keep the safety model intact"
    lead="From CONTRIBUTING.md. A change to worker execution, approval, sandboxing or re-verification comes with a test that proves the failure path."
  >
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <Card title="Failure paths to prove" icon="shield-check" tone="green">
        <ul class="plain">
          <li>With no usable sandbox, a sandbox-mode worker does not start.</li>
          <li>An unconfirmed re-verification blocks auto-landing.</li>
          <li>A denied approval grants nothing.</li>
          <li>A worker does not change your checkout or your default branch.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
      <Card title="In the pull request" icon="git-pull-request">
        <ul class="plain">
          <li>What changed, and how you verified it.</li>
          <li>Any safety boundary it touches.</li>
          <li>Known limits and follow-up work.</li>
          <li>Docs that say what is enforced, what is policy and what is planned.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    band
    narrow
    eyebrow="Where to help"
    title="The most useful contributions"
    lead="skep has one maintainer and few outside contributors so far, so a careful report counts for a lot."
  >
    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <Card title="Challenge the security model" icon="search" href="/security.html">
        Read the invariants and the sandbox profiles and try to break a claim. A hole is the most
        valuable issue you can file. Report anything exploitable privately.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Report what breaks on your machine" icon="laptop" href={ISSUES_URL}>
        Another distribution, another kernel, another macOS release. Include
        <code>skep --version</code> and the output of <code>skep doctor</code>.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Sandbox backends and hardening" icon="box" href="/docs/sandboxing.html">
        bubblewrap, Landlock and Seatbelt hardening, and backends that do not exist yet: Docker,
        micro-VMs.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Worker adapters" icon="plug" href="/docs/workers.html">
        One documented contract lets another agent run under the same supervision. The adapter is
        small because skep owns supervision, not code generation.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Correct the docs" icon="file-text" href="/docs.html">
        If a page claims more than the code does, that is a bug. Every doc page links to its source
        on GitHub.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Conduct" icon="heart-handshake" href="/code-of-conduct.html">
        Critique code and ideas, not people. The code of conduct applies to issues and pull
        requests.
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section narrow eyebrow="Repository" title="Current state" lead="Read from GitHub and PyPI when the page loads.">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="t-micro">Latest release</dt>
        <dd><a href={PYPI_URL}><span data-version>v{version}</span></a> on PyPI</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="t-micro">Last push</dt>
        <dd><span data-stat="pushed">see GitHub</span></dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="t-micro">Open issues and pull requests</dt>
        <dd><a href={ISSUES_URL}><span data-stat="issues">see GitHub</span></a></dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="t-micro">Licence</dt>
        <dd>MIT</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="t-small c-mut" style="margin-top: var(--s-4)">
      GitHub counts open pull requests in that number. If the request fails, the values above are
      the ones the page was built with.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  .plain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--s-5);
  }
  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s-4);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .facts div {
    padding: var(--s-4) var(--s-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-card);
    border-radius: var(--r-card);
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }
  .facts dt {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .facts dd {
    margin: var(--s-2) 0 0;
    color: var(--text-bright);
  }
</style>
